{% extends '@layout/default.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}{{ article.title }}{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
    <div class="page-intro">
        <div class="container">
            <p class="welcome">Knowledge Base</p>
            <h1>DotKernel & Zend Framework</h1>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="container py-5">
        <div class="row justify-content-center">
            <div class="col-lg-8">

                <div class="mb-5">
                    <h4 class="fw-bold">1. What is a framework?</h4>
                    <p class="text-muted">A standard library of components that you can drop into your application.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="mb-5">
                    <h4 class="fw-bold">2. What is Zend Framework (ZF)?</h4>
                    <p class="text-muted">A PHP library for building web applications. It is a hybrid framework that can be used in a large range of projects, and at its core is an implementation of the <strong>Model-View-Controller (MVC)</strong> pattern.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="mb-5">
                    <h4 class="fw-bold">3. Why is ZF different from other frameworks?</h4>
                    <p class="text-muted">ZF is the only major framework for PHP 5 that uses the very business-friendly <strong>New BSD license</strong>.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="mb-5">
                    <h4 class="fw-bold">4. Why use ZF?</h4>
                    <ul class="text-muted">
                        <li><strong>Everything in the box</strong> — includes MVC, authentication, searching, localization, PDF creation, email, and web services.</li>
                        <li><strong>Modern design</strong> — written in object-oriented PHP5 using design patterns.</li>
                        <li><strong>Easy to learn</strong> — each component can be used without understanding its entirety.</li>
                        <li><strong>Simpler &amp; Rapid Development</strong></li>
                    </ul>
                    <blockquote class="blockquote border-start border-primary ps-3 mt-3">
                        <p class="fst-italic text-muted">"We chose Zend Framework because it had a committed company behind it, a strong community, and because it would continue to evolve and become an increasingly standard code base."</p>
                    </blockquote>
                </div>

                <div class="mb-5">
                    <h4 class="fw-bold">5. Advantages of ZF</h4>
                    <ul class="text-muted">
                        <li><strong>Flexibility</strong> — allows you to pull out individual pieces and use them independently.</li>
                        <li>Very little interdependence between components.</li>
                        <li>Easy to embed into another framework.</li>
                        <li>Can use alternative template systems.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="mb-5">
                    <h4 class="fw-bold">6. Disadvantages of ZF</h4>
                    <p class="text-muted">Requires PHP 5.1.4 minimally due to object-oriented interfaces. Upgrading to PHP 5.2.1 or later is recommended for performance and security improvements.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="mb-5">
                    <h4 class="fw-bold">7. Why create DotKernel?</h4>
                    <p class="text-muted">To create a simple-to-use framework for easier internal development, where the entire team works to the same standard.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="mb-5">
                    <h4 class="fw-bold">8 &amp; 9. Advantages &amp; Disadvantages of DotKernel</h4>
                    <ul class="text-muted">
                        <li>Every new team member must learn the framework.</li>
                        <li>May contain projection bugs not caught early.</li>
                        <li>Loss of time and money.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="mb-5">
                    <h4 class="fw-bold">10. Can DotKernel and ZF be combined?</h4>
                    <p class="text-muted">Yes — individual ZF components like database access, search engine, or feed client can be used alongside DotKernel without migrating the entire application at once.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="mb-5">
                    <h4 class="fw-bold">11 &amp; 12. Combined Advantages &amp; Disadvantages</h4>
                    <ul class="text-muted">
                        <li><strong>Advantage:</strong> The simplicity of DotKernel combined with the power, flexibility, and community support of ZF.</li>
                        <li><strong>Disadvantage:</strong> Loss of time and money.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="mt-5 pt-3 border-top">
                    <h6 class="fw-bold text-muted text-uppercase small">Bibliography</h6>
                    <ol class="text-muted small">
                        <li><a href="http://downloads.zend.com/static/topics/qa_zf_webinar.pdf" target="_blank">Zend Framework Q&amp;A Webinar PDF</a></li>
                        <li>"php|architect's Guide to Programming with Zend Framework" — Cal Evans</li>
                        <li>"Zend Framework in action" — Rob Allen, Nick Lo</li>
                        <li><a href="http://framework.zend.com/whyzf/ZF-Jolt-Award-Nomination.ppt" target="_blank">ZF Jolt Award Nomination PPT</a></li>
                    </ol>
                </div>

            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
{% endblock %}